WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.920
Most financial plans are complicated because the writers are confused or lying.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:13.520
This one is complicated because you're trying to convince one hundred and ninety-five countries to voluntarily stop buying

3
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.840
so many weapons. While paying your investors forever. That actually IS complicated.

4
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:26.480
But the architecture is embarrassingly simple: one. Raise one billion dollars.

5
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:36.320
two. Spend it to pass the treaty. three. Manage twenty-seven point two billion dollars plus annually forever.

6
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:44.000
Three pillars. That's it. Pillar one: Fundraising (Raise one billion dollars).

7
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:53.820
Instrument: VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds. Returns are two hundred and seventy-two percent annually, which looks like

8
00:00:53.820 --> 00:01:00.680
a typo but is just arithmetic: two point seven two billion dollars divided by one billion dollars.

9
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.860
The structure is senior debt, meaning the fund pays bondholders before anyone else, like a V.I.P.

10
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:16.820
line, but for not dying. Payout is ten percent of all treaty inflows, which equals two point seven two billion dollars per

11
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:23.920
year. Collateral is a twenty-seven point two billion dollar plus annual treaty revenue stream.

12
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:31.660
The pitch is "Invest one billion dollars, get two point seven two billion dollars per year, forever."

13
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.980
Every Ponzi scheme in history has promised returns like this.

14
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:41.860
The difference is that this one has collateral: the entire military budget of the planet.

15
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.880
Which, unlike Bernie Madoff's spreadsheet, actually exists.

16
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.740
Timeline: twelve to twenty-four months to raise one billion dollars.

17
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.740
Why bonds, not donations? Donations require generosity. Returns require greed.

18
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:07.620
Greed is faster. Why one billion dollars? Pharma lobbying (roughly three hundred million dollars a year) plus the military-industrial

19
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:15.140
complex (roughly one hundred and fifty million dollars a year) equals four hundred and fifty million dollars a year spent

20
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.440
specifically to keep things exactly as terrible as they are.

21
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.680
That's the price of the status quo, renewed annually, like a subscription to suffering.

22
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:34.600
Raise one billion dollars ONCE to pass a treaty that generates twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year FOREVER.

23
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:43.020
Over a realistic ten-year campaign, opposition spends roughly four point five billion dollars to your one billion dollars.

24
00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:50.760
They outspend you four point five to one. But their four hundred and fifty million dollars a year is spread across maintaining

25
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:58.420
thousands of contracts, subsidizing dozens of incumbents, and defending the entire status quo.

26
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.000
Your one billion dollars is a single bullet aimed at a single treaty.

27
00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.640
Concentrated offense beats diffuse defense. Ask any general.

28
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.360
(They'd know; you're paying them enough.). Nobody gets paid unless it works.

29
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:19.040
Lawyers get success-based compensation. Team compensation is mostly equity.

30
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:26.080
This is how you know it's not a government program. In the Seed phase, two hundred and fifty to four hundred million dollars

31
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:34.080
is spent on the platform, policy frameworks, and pilots to reach fifty million verified participants, with a difficulty

32
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:43.100
level of "Convince a few billionaires". In the Series A phase, five hundred million to one billion dollars is spent to scale

33
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:51.340
to G Seven nations, targeting three point five percent global participation, or two hundred and eighty million people, with

34
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:59.620
a difficulty level of "Convince several countries". In the Growth phase, five hundred million to one point one billion dollars

35
00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:07.420
is spent on the ratification push in major powers, leading to the first one billion dollar disbursement, with a difficulty

36
00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:14.440
level of "Convince the countries with nukes". Ranges reflect scenario planning (optimistic to pessimistic).

37
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:22.360
The one billion dollar target is the base case; upper bounds cover contingencies because convincing nuclear powers to behave

38
00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:29.860
rationally occasionally requires overtime. Pillar two: Campaign Budget (Spend one billion dollars).

39
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:39.580
Detailed Breakdown: Campaign Budget. The one-time "activation energy" to convince eight billion people to marginally reduce

40
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:48.440
their commitment to mutual annihilation. Think of it as a Kickstarter for not going extinct: Global Referendum: two hundred

41
00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:55.660
and fifty million dollars for the viral referral system, two hundred and eighty million votes, and platform development.

42
00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.460
Political Lobbying: six hundred and fifty million dollars for A.I.

43
00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:11.180
targeted campaigns in the U.S., E.U., and G Twenty, Super PACs, and military-industrial complex, or M.I.C., conversion,

44
00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:21.100
outspending pharma and the M.I.C. Reserve Fund: one hundred million dollars for post-passage transition and a contingency

45
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:31.200
buffer. Key Innovations. Viral mechanics: twenty cents per vote (an optimistic lower bound with a base case of fifty to

46
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:37.140
eighty-nine cents) versus the five to fifteen dollar traditional cost per voter.

47
00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:45.980
Democracy at wholesale prices. Strategic focus: twenty high-impact countries, not all one hundred and ninety-five.

48
00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:55.000
You don't need Liechtenstein's permission. Timeline: thirty-six to sixty months (optimistic) to one hundred and twenty plus

49
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:04.360
months (realistic, given that you're humans). The landmine ban took six years, and that was just agreeing not to hide explosives

50
00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.440
in the ground, which you'd think wouldn't require a treaty at all.

51
00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.940
You're betting on viral referendum mechanics and existing disarmament frameworks.

52
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:21.200
Also on the assumption that your species wants to survive, which, based on my four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven

53
00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:30.780
years of observation, remains an open question. Pillar 3: Treasury (Manage twenty-seven point two billion dollars or more

54
00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:39.640
per year). Once the treaty passes, twenty-seven point two billion dollars flows annually from "how to kill people" budgets

55
00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:45.080
into "how to stop people dying" budgets. Same money, opposite direction.

56
00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.340
One hundred plus nations contribute one percent of military budgets.

57
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:57.480
Not because they've had a moral awakening. Because the math makes non-compliance look like setting money on fire, which,

58
00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:02.280
to be fair, is also what military budgets do, just with more steps.

59
00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:11.000
The Incentive Alignment Bond mechanism makes compliance self-enforcing: politicians in non-compliant countries watch their

60
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:19.820
Public Good Scores drop, voters abandon them for someone who can do basic arithmetic, and compliance becomes the career-optimal

61
00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:26.000
choice. You don't need politicians to be good. You just need them to be selfish in the right direction.

62
00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:32.520
How the Money Gets Spent: eighty ten ten. The allocation is automatic.

63
00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:38.920
No committees. No debates. No one arguing for six months about font choices on the grant application.

64
00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:46.680
Just math, executing silently, like a competent employee (something most of your governments have never experienced).

65
00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:55.320
Clinical Trials and Platform: eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, for patient subsidies, research,

66
00:07:55.460 --> 00:08:04.020
and the platform. VICTORY Bond Returns: ten percent, or two point seven two billion dollars, for perpetual investor payments.

67
00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:13.420
I.A.B. Political Incentives: ten percent, or two point seven two billion dollars, for rewards for supporting legislators.

68
00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:18.570
The twenty percent for bonds and political incentives is sacred and untouchable.

69
00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:25.440
Smart contracts distribute both automatically. No human hands involved, which, given your track record with money, is a

70
00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:33.539
feature. Touch them and the system dies. The remaining eighty percent (twenty-one point eight billion dollars per year)

71
00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:38.419
funds clinical trials and research infrastructure. Wishocracy governs the allocation.

72
00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:48.120
Dynamic Patient Subsidies. The revolutionary part: instead of paying researchers to write about diseases, you pay patients

73
00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.960
to participate in curing them. I know. Paying the people who are actually sick.

74
00:08:54.340 --> 00:09:02.360
What a concept. Patients organize to grow the treasury (because for once, participating in their own survival is financially

75
00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:09.810
rewarded). Researchers compete to attract patients (instead of competing to write the most persuasive grant application,

76
00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:16.180
which is a different skill entirely from doing science, in the same way that writing a good restaurant review is a different

77
00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:23.420
skill from cooking). Insurance companies save money (trials are cheaper than paying for decades of chronic suffering).

78
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:30.600
Compare that to N.I.H. grants: six months of proposal writing, committees deciding whether the proposal writing was good

79
00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:40.340
enough, universities skimming forty percent overhead, and then maybe, possibly, if the stars align and no one retires mid-review,

80
00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:49.320
some science. Related Documents. VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds: Investment thesis and bond structure.

81
00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:57.120
Campaign Budget: Detailed line-item breakdown. Investor Risk Analysis: Risk factors and mitigations.

82
00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.280
Defense Contractor Alignment: Defense contractor conversion strategy.

83
00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.480
Cost-Benefit Analysis for a Decentralized Drug Assessment Framework: Complete R.O.I.

84
00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:16.920
analysis. The math: Invest one billion dollars. Get two point seven two billion dollars per year.

85
00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:24.860
Forever. Your species spent approximately five billion dollars on a particle accelerator to find a boson.

86
00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:29.360
This costs less and finds a cure for dying.
